  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C09-somos",
      "sequence": 952,
      "title": "somos — \"we are\"",
      "headword": "somos",
      "romanization": "somos",
      "gloss": "\"we are\" — from a different Latin verb than soy, and that is why it looks nothing like it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5ef7d493ef0444f5",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"I am.\" (Soy.) And \"we speak.\" (Hablamos.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the -mos survives even here",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "somos — we are."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at what survived. Ser is the most irregular verb in Spanish — soy, eres, es share almost nothing — and yet the plural still ends in -mos, exactly as hablamos and comemos do."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That ending is the single most stable thing in the language. Even a verb that has thrown away everything else kept it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And here is why ser's forms refuse to resemble each other: it is not one verb. It is two Latin verbs fused into a single paradigm."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin esse, \"to be\", gave soy and somos — the s- forms, and the rest of that family arrives over the next two lessons."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin sedēre, \"to sit\", gave ser itself, and the future and conditional."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So somos and ser are not irregular versions of each other. They are descendants of different words, brought together because Spanish needed one verb where Latin had two."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English did something very similar. Am, is, are, was, be — no two of those look related, because they are not: they come from three separate Old English verbs. Every language seems to build \"to be\" out of spare parts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"hablamos, comemos, somos\" — the same ending, three times",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"hablamos, comemos, somos\" — the same ending, three times]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C09-somos-form",
              "prompt": "Say we are.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-SER-PRESENT-1PL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "somos",
                "nosotros somos"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Yes — and it still ends in -mos.",
                "incorrect": "somos."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C09-somos-why",
              "prompt": "Why do ser's forms look so unlike each other?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-SER"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "it is two latin verbs fused",
                "two verbs",
                "esse and sedere",
                "it comes from two verbs"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Right — esse and sedere, brought into one paradigm.",
                "incorrect": "Because it is two Latin verbs fused: esse and sedere."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is \"we are\"? (Somos.) Which ending did even this verb keep? (-mos.) And why do soy and ser look unrelated? (They are unrelated — two Latin verbs in one paradigm.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C09-son",
      "sequence": 954,
      "title": "son — \"they are\"",
      "headword": "son",
      "romanization": "son",
      "gloss": "\"they are,\" and \"ustedes are\" — the most common verb form in Spanish",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:840bbc643d10cc33",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"we are.\" (Somos.) And \"they speak.\" (Hablan.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the final -n, again",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "son — they are, and ustedes son, you all are."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three letters, and it is one of the two or three commonest words in the whole language. The rule you know holds even here: a final -n means the subject is plural and is not you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin's form was sunt. Spanish dropped the final -t, exactly as it dropped it from hablat to give habla, and sunt became son."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is worth noticing because it is the same erosion you have already watched happen twice. Irregular verbs are not exempt from sound change; they are simply verbs where sound change ran on material that had already been strange."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word you may already know. English sonata, sonnet and sound have nothing to do with this — they come from Latin sonāre, \"to sound\". Son the verb and son the noun in English are unrelated to each other and to both. Spanish is full of these collisions, and the way through is always the same: the root, not the spelling."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"somos, son\" — the two plural forms you now hold",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"somos, son\" — the two plural forms you now hold]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"ustedes son\", addressing a group",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"ustedes son\", addressing a group]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C09-son-form",
              "prompt": "Say they are.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-SER-PRESENT-3PL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 6,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "son",
                "ellos son",
                "ustedes son"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Yes.",
                "incorrect": "son."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C09-son-latin",
              "prompt": "What Latin form is son worn down from?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-SER-PRESENT-3PL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "sunt",
                "sunt, minus the t"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Yes — sunt, minus the final -t.",
                "incorrect": "sunt."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is \"they are\"? (Son.) What did Latin's sunt lose? (Its -t, like every other final -t.) Who else does son address? (Ustedes.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C09-sois",
      "sequence": 956,
      "title": "sois — the last of the six",
      "headword": "sois",
      "romanization": "sois",
      "gloss": "the vosotros form of ser — Spain's, and the last of the six",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:55151a9fc2ad27a1",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"they are.\" (Son.) And which region uses vosotros? (Spain.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: one syllable, and the paradigm closes",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vosotros sois — you all are, in Spain."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One syllable, and with it the six forms of ser are complete: soy, eres, es, somos, sois, son."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "As before, this is the form you need in order to follow Spain rather than to speak everywhere. In the Americas, ustedes son does the same job."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin had estis. Spanish wore away the e- at the front and the -t- in the middle, and sois is what was left — a word reduced almost to nothing but still doing its whole job."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the pattern with this verb throughout. Ser is short everywhere because it is said constantly, and constant use grinds words down. The commonest verb in a language is almost always its most eroded one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "HEAR",
              "instruction": "\"sois\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU HEAR: \"sois\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "becomes Spain, addressing friends."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"ustedes son\" then \"vosotros sois\" — the same sentence, two continents",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"ustedes son\" then \"vosotros sois\" — the same sentence, two continents]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
