    {
      "id": "ES-C09-repaso-ser",
      "sequence": 958,
      "title": "Review — all of ser",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "review — the whole of ser, and why no two of its forms look alike",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b2030155460523b1",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nothing new. Say \"we are\", then \"they are.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the table, and why it looks like that",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "who",
                "ser, which gives:"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 6,
              "utterances": [
                "who: yo. ser, which gives:: soy.",
                "who: tú. ser, which gives:: eres.",
                "who: él, ella, usted. ser, which gives:: es.",
                "who: nosotros. ser, which gives:: somos.",
                "who: vosotros. ser, which gives:: sois.",
                "who: ellos, ustedes. ser, which gives:: son."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Most books present this as a table to memorise. You have built it a form at a time, and you can now read something off it that memorisation would never give you."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five of the six begin with s-. One does not. Eres is the outlier, and it is the outlier because it comes from somewhere else — Latin eris, from esse's own future stem, pulled in to fill a gap. The rest are the s- family: sum, sumus, sunt."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the \"irregularity\" is not chaos. It is a seam between two verbs, and you can see exactly where it runs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
